Diffusion Models: Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models (DDPMs) have shown great potential in various computer vision tasks. DDPMs consist of two main phases: the forward
(or diffusion) process and the reverse (or denoising) process as summarized in Figure 4 [30, 31, 32, 23]. In the forward process, a white noise is gradually added to a real image until it becomes a noisy image with a standard normal distribution. The intermediate latent variables are denoted as z1, z2, . . . , zT . Specifically, at the first step, the noisy image z1 is obtained as:
z1 = p1 − β1 · x + pβ1 · ε1 (5)
